%I A400174 #42 Oct 21 2026 10:31:02
%S A400174 1,2,3,3,3,3,2,4,4,3,3,5,8,5,4,7,4,4,6,6,3,6,4,8,5,2,5,8,11,7,4,10,13,
%T A400174 4,7,10,13,9,3,9,12,3,6,6,9,12,5,8,11,8,11,11,5,5,5,8,5,4,14,7,10,4,7,
%U A400174 10,10,4,4,7,7,7,4,5,7,6,6,9,19,12,6,9
%N A400174 Number of steps starting from 2*n-1 until the first repeated value under the map k -> (5*k-1)/2^v_2(5*k-1) if k == 1 (mod 4) and k -> (5*k+1)/2^v_2(5*k+1) if k == 3 (mod 4) where v_2(k) = A007814(k); or -1 if no value ever repeats.
%C A400174 The known cycles are the fixed point 1 and the three-cycle 7 -> 9 -> 11 -> 7.
%C A400174 If a trajectory has preperiod length mu and eventual cycle length lambda, then a(n) = mu + lambda.
%H A400174 Farhad Banazadeh, <a href="/A400174/b400174.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..10000</a>
%H A400174 Farhad Banazadeh, <a href="https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.22709065">Ternary (5k +- 1)/4 Collatz-Type Map with Two Observed Attractors</a>, Zenodo, 2026.
%e A400174 For n = 1, the orbit begins 1, 1. The first repeated value occurs after 1 iteration, so a(1) = 1.
%e A400174 For n = 2, the orbit begins 3, 1, 1. The first repeated value occurs after 2 iterations, so a(2) = 2.
%e A400174 For n = 4, the orbit begins 7, 9, 11, 7. The first repeated value occurs after 3 iterations, so a(4) = 3.
%t A400174 A400174[n_] := Length[NestWhileList[#/2^IntegerExponent[#, 2] & [5*# + If[Mod[#, 4] == 1, -1, 1]] &, 2*n - 1, UnsameQ, All]] - 1;
%t A400174 Array[A400174, 100] (* _Paolo Xausa_, Oct 21 2026 *)
%o A400174 (Python)
%o A400174 def T(k):
%o A400174     y = 5*k + (-1 if k % 4 == 1 else 1)
%o A400174     while y % 2 == 0:
%o A400174         y //= 2
%o A400174     return y
%o A400174 def a(n):
%o A400174     k, seen = 2*n - 1, set()
%o A400174     while k not in seen:
%o A400174         seen.add(k)
%o A400174         k = T(k)
%o A400174     return len(seen)
%Y A400174 Cf. A005408, A006666, A007814, A090691, A133420, A401143.
%K A400174 nonn,changed
%O A400174 1,2
%A A400174 _Farhad Banazadeh_, Sep 20 2026
